A||N||6||Carrolles||1.6:1 rockers on stock head||||1
Z||000000||Carrolles||09-14-1999||11:19 AM||||Hi,<P>I'm rebuilding my 2.8L in my 85SE and would like to install 1.6:1 rockers.  I read in the online service guide that this is possible and that the rockers are the ones available for the small block chevy.  I looked in the performance parts book at the pontiac dealership and found the 1.6:1 rockers but they are aluminum rollers.  Has anyone tried this?  If so what were the results?  Part numbers?<P>I will port match the heads and port the exhaust and intake manifolds also.<P>I recently had to replace a car that one of my daughters totaled, so funds are tight.  I'm trying to salvage as much as I can from this motor.<P>This project started out as a clutch replacement.  It evolved into a rebuild when I pulled the valve covers and intake to replace leaky gaskets ane found gobs of coked sludge.  My timing chain is also badly stretched.  I guess the previous owner wasn't too good at oil changes for the first 110,000 miles.  It is amazing that the engine was running great with all of this sludge.<P>Thanks,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000001||Dale||09-14-1999||02:01 PM||||Boy that sounds like me , go to change the head gasket and you end up rebuilding the whole thing and of course I cant leave anything stock.  It just wouldn't be right.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||09-14-1999||07:32 PM||||Here are the exact part numbers from Summit Racing Equipment( <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com)" TARGET=_blank>http://www.summitracing.com)</A>  for the Competition Cams 1.6:1 ratio rockers, correct springs, and recommended pushrods for this modification.<P><BR>CCA-1414-12  	ROCKER ARMS 			$133.99<P>CCA-980-12   	VALVE SPRINGS			$ 38.69<P>CCA-7816-12  	PUSHRODS CHEVY V-6 173  	$ 29.69<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||09-14-1999||07:47 PM||||If you have your motor out being rebuilt, I  would just install a higher lift cam rather then the higher ratio rockers.  It should actually be cheaper, and may even be somewhat better for the valve train in the long run.  If you still want additional improvements to the valve train beyond that of the cam, I would suggest running roller tip 1.5:1 ratio or even the full roller gold race rockers from Crane.  I would not advise the use of 1.6 rockers with a high lift cam!!<BR>The valve springs and pushrods would be highly recommended no matter what method you decide on.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||squid||09-14-1999||10:09 PM||||Aluminum rockers for small block chevys are too wide for the 2.8. Competition Cams 1.6 rockers for the 2.8 that are just roller tipped will fit and clear the valve covers. Thats what I have on my '85 but I had to shorten the valve guide bosses because of the amount of lift my cam created with those rockers. Get the Chevy Power book from your GM dealer. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||09-15-1999||02:48 AM||||I should have been more specific on this, my bad.  The Crane Cams part number 25750-12 Gold Race full roller rocker arms are a special design with a narrower body for 60° V6 applications.  The 25759-12 are the same but have a 1.6:1 ratio.  At any rate, they cost way more than I could ever justify spending on rocker arms. :-)<P>For more info on these somewhat overpriced parts see:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com/rocker.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cranecams.com/rocker.htm</A> <P>and<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com/master/apps/chevy1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cranecams.com/master/apps/chevy1.htm</A> <P>Mike||||reg||9
Z||000006||batboy||09-15-1999||09:04 PM||||I agree with Mwbackus about if you're doing a rebuild, replace the cam with the lift you want. Cams should always be replaced during a rebuild anyway. Squid's right about most of the V8 aluminum true roller rockers being too wide for the 60 degree V6, plus they are very expensive.||||reg||1
